  <h2>Nobody walks in as the problem</h2>
  <p>The most common worry we hear before a first session is some version of "she's going to think it's all my fault." Both of you tend to arrive with it, which should tell you something. We are not here to decide who started it, and there is no scoreboard at the end of the hour.</p>
  <p>What we are here for is the pattern between you, because that is the thing that keeps producing the same evening over and over. Once the two of you can see it running, you stop being opponents and start being two people looking at the same problem from the same side of the table.</p>
  <p>You also do not both have to be ready at once. Plenty of this work starts with one person and <a href="/therapy/individuals">a few individual sessions</a>, sorting out what you actually want to say before anyone says it. When you are ready, the free call is a good place to ask what this would look like for the two of you.</p>
